<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">White Horde</h1><p class="st-meta">1226 CE – 1428 CE (approx.) · capital Sighnaq</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern wing of the Golden Horde, ruled by descendants of Jochi's son Orda on the steppes of Kazakhstan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Jochi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan's</a> eldest son, died in 1227, his lands were divided. His eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orda-khan">Orda</a> received the eastern part, the left wing of the ulus, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-river">Ural River</a>, with its own line of khans subordinate to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. Names of the 'White' and 'Blue' wings are used inconsistently in the sources.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The khans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orda-khan">Orda</a> line ruled nomadic Turkic and Mongol tribes in the steppes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syr-darya">Syr Darya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irtysh">Irtysh</a>, with towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sighnaq">Sighnaq</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sauran">Sauran</a> on the Syr Darya. The Orda khans enjoyed considerable autonomy and sent their own envoys to the Yuan and Ilkhanid courts.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1378 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>, backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a>, made himself khan of the White Horde, and by 1380 he had seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a> and reunited it with the rest of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>. After further civil wars the eastern lands passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids">Shibanid</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jochids">Jochid</a> princes, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abul-khayr-khan">Abu'l-Khayr</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/uzbek-khanate">Uzbek Khanate</a> in 1428.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/white-horde.jpg" alt="White Horde" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Afil · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Horde_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1226 CE – 1428 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sighnaq</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Orda, Urus Khan, Tokhtamysh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism, later Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kipchak Turkic, Mongolian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ulus of Orda, Ak Orda, Eastern wing of the Golden Horde</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1327&amp;focus=white-horde" aria-label="White Horde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6280 747 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where White Horde lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6600 1003L6352 637M6600 1003L6409 496"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M6409 496h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6600 1003h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6600 1003h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1327&amp;focus=white-horde">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
